{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J5"],
      "pins": ["J5.4", "J5.7"],
      "nets": ["NET0161"],
      "observed": "On the CM4 sheet, the HDMI receptacle J5 carries the same net name NET0161 on two different TMDS pins: pin 4 (D1-) and pin 7 (CK+). The same NET0161 also appears on the module's HDMI_TX1_N pin, so the connector's data‑1‑ line and the clock+ line are tied together (and the clock‑ pin is likewise mis‑mapped onto a data pair net).",
      "expected": "Each of the four HDMI TMDS differential pairs (D2, D1, D0, CLK) must be its own independent net; D1- and CLK+ must never share a net. The specification is silent on HDMI, so this is judged by standard practice for a TMDS interface.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "CM4 sheet, HDMI connector J5 left pin column: pin4 'D1-' label 'NET0161' and pin7 'CK+' label 'NET0161' (same net on two distinct pairs)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "The specification lists only GPIO, Ethernet, USB, camera connectors and the CAN/e‑stop/LED architecture; it does not document any HDMI shorting as a known issue, so this is not a pre‑existing property."}
      ],
      "impact": "Shorting the data‑1‑ line to the clock+ line (and mis‑routing the clock pair) corrupts the TMDS signalling; the HDMI output would not produce a valid video signal and could load the module's differential drivers.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build otherwise matches the spec (CAN crystals are 16 MHz, LTC4412 ideal‑diode and ISL54222 analog switches are present, e‑stop open‑drain sinks feed the CAN connectors, WS2812 string and TVS/Ethernet protection are in place, and the documented open issues are not counted). The one deviation readable off the drawing is a shorted pair on the HDMI connector, where J5 pin 4 (D1-) and pin 7 (CK+) are both on net NET0161."
}

